  "question": {
    "text": "The frequency and the intensity of incident beam of light falling on the surface of a photoelectric material is increased by a factor of two. This will"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "increase the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected photoelectrons by a factor of more than two and would increase the photoelectric current by a factor of two.",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "increase the maximum kinetic energy of the photo electron&nbsp;and would increase the photoelectric current both by a factor of two.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "increase the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectron by a factor of two and will have no effect on the magnitude of the photoelectric current produced.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "not produce any effect on the kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons but will increase the photoelectric current by a factor of two.",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Using Einstein&#39;s photoelectric equations,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>Thus, when the frequency of incident light radiation (photons) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> increases to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>v</mi></math>, the stopping potential <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math> changes to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi>ii</mi></mfenced></math></p><p>On dividing Eq. (ii) by Eq. (ii), we get</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>&#981;</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>'</mo></msup><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#8658;</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>'</mo></msubsup><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></p>"
  }
}